Yes, Spring does map key values for input from a client in the form of a JSON object when using the `@RequestBody` annotation in the method parameters of a controller.

The `@RequestBody` annotation is used to bind the HTTP request body (usually containing JSON or XML) with a method parameter (usually a Java POJO) in a Spring web application. The Spring framework will automatically convert the incoming JSON data to the Java object, mapping the JSON keys to the object properties.

Here's an example to demonstrate this:

Suppose you have a Java class called `User`:

```java
public class User {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    // Getters and setters
}
```

And you have a Spring controller end-point for receiving user data:

```java
@RestController
public class UserController {

    @PostMapping("/users")
    public ResponseEntity<User> createUser(@RequestBody User user) {
        // Process the user object here
        return ResponseEntity.ok(user);
    }
}
```

When you send a JSON object to the `/users` endpoint, Spring will map the key-value pairs in the JSON object to the `User` object's properties. For example, if you send the following JSON object:

```json
{
    "name": "John Doe",
    "age": 30
}
```

Spring will automatically create a `User` object, set its properties from the JSON data, and provide it as the `user` parameter in the `createUser` method.

