In Java, it is not possible to have a concrete class with a generic `SELF` directly. The reason is that Java does not support higher-kinded types, which are needed to represent generic self-referencing types like `SELF`.

However, you can achieve a similar effect by using interfaces and type parameters with the `<?>` wildcard. Here's an example:
```java
public interface Animal {
    void makeSound();
}

public class Dog implements Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}

public class Cat implements Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Meow!");
    }
}

public interface GenericAnimal<SELF extends Animal & GenericAnimal<SELF>> {
    void makeSound();

    default SELF reproduce() {
        try {
            @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
            SELF newAnimal = (SELF) instantiateClass();
            return newAnimal;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
            return null;
        }
    }

    protected abstract Animal instantiateClass() throws Exception;
}

public class ConcreteAnimalFactory {
    public static <T extends Animal & GenericAnimal<T>> T createAnimal(T animal) {
        return animal.reproduce();
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog dog = new Dog();
        Cat cat = new Cat();

        Dog reproducedDog = ConcreteAnimalFactory.createAnimal(dog);
        Cat reproducedCat = ConcreteAnimalFactory.createAnimal(cat);

        reproducedDog.makeSound();
        reproducedCat.makeSound();
    }
}
```
In this example, we have an `Animal` interface and two concrete classes `Dog` and `Cat` that implement it. We also have a `GenericAnimal` interface with a methods `makeSound` and `reproduce`. The `reproduce` method is a default method, which means it is implemented in the concrete classes that extend `GenericAnimal`.

The `GenericAnimal` interface has a type parameter `SELF` that extends both `Animal` and `GenericAnimal<SELF>`. This allows us to have a generic self-referencing type in the sense that the same type parameter is used in both the extends clause and the type of the `reproduce` method.

Finally, we have a `ConcreteAnimalFactory` class that has a static `createAnimal` method, which takes an `Animal` instance and returns a new instance of the same type. The method simply calls the `reproduce` method on the given `Animal` instance.

In summary, while it's not possible to have a concrete class with a generic `SELF` in Java, you can achieve a similar effect using interfaces, type parameters, and default methods.

